# Root scope for the service worker of whichever app is served at "/".
#
# An app's worker lives in its own directory, so its default scope is that
# directory and it cannot control the root page. This header raises the
# allowed scope; the registration in that app's HTML asks for it.
#
# RULE: only the app currently at "/" gets this. The other app keeps its
# own-directory scope, which still wins for pages under its path because the
# longest matching scope controls a client. Two workers cannot both own "/".
#
# Halloween is at "/" — after the mode switch, move this to
# /southbysouthwest/service-worker.js and update both registrations.

/halloween/service-worker.js
  Service-Worker-Allowed: /

# The festival worker keeps this too, even though it no longer serves "/".
# Phase 1 registered it AT scope "/", and that scope is re-validated on every
# update check — drop the header and those registrations can never update,
# leaving the device pinned to a cache-first worker still serving the old
# root page. With the header they can update, and the new version stands
# down (see its activate handler).
/southbysouthwest/service-worker.js
  Service-Worker-Allowed: /
